- `action(n1, "start")`: Hallucinates a procedural step; the source text begins directly with "prepare yourself to cut the banana" without any preliminary "start" action.
- `action(n5, "end of process")`: Hallucinates an action node; the source describes termination as a condition ("the process will come to an end"), which is already semantically complete with `end(e1)`.
- `precedence(i1, n1)`: Breaks source chronology by routing from the initial node to the hallucinated `n1` instead of the first documented step (`n2`); the text states "To begin the process, prepare yourself...".
- `precedence(n5, e1)`: Disrupts the intended progression by inserting `n5` before the terminal node; the source explicitly states "Once the banana has been cut, the process will come to an end," implying direct flow from cutting to termination.